1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:04,920
N.I.H. Fails to Institute Health The National Institutes of Health spends ninety-six point seven percent of its budget on

2
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:13,080
everything except testing whether drugs work in humans. It's like a fire department that spends ninety-six point seven percent

3
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:18,000
of its budget on fire-themed calendars and three point three percent on water.

4
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:23,840
The Allocation Scandal. Fifty-five million people die annually.

5
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:30,900
The National Institutes of Health has an annual budget of approximately forty-seven billion dollars.

6
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:34,960
You would think those two facts would be related. They are not.

7
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:43,820
Of two hundred and forty-seven billion dollars the N.I.H. spent over a decade, exactly three point three percent went to

8
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:51,040
testing whether treatments actually work in humans. The other ninety-six point seven percent went to everything else.

9
00:00:51,620 --> 00:01:00,580
"Everything else" includes understanding disease mechanisms, translating discoveries, training scientists, maintaining buildings,

10
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:08,740
and running a program called "All of Us" that cost two point one six billion dollars to observe people without treating

11
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:14,270
them, which is called "research" the way watching someone drown from a boat is called "lifeguarding.".

12
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:22,880
That might be defensible if we'd already tested all the known safe compounds that are piled up waiting for human testing.

13
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:31,420
We haven't. There are roughly nine thousand five hundred compounds with established safety profiles, and ninety-nine point

14
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:36,200
seven percent of their possible disease applications have never been tested.

15
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:41,680
The pipeline is overflowing with candidates. The bottleneck is trials.

16
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:48,110
The drugs are sitting there. Safe. Ready. Waiting. They've been waiting for decades.

17
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:51,880
Some of them have been waiting longer than most of your scientists have been alive.

18
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:59,440
It's like spending ninety-six point seven percent of your grocery budget on cookbooks and three point three percent on food,

19
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:07,420
then wondering why you're starving. Except the cookbooks are very prestigious and the starving people aren't on the committee

20
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:12,300
that decides whether to buy more cookbooks. This isn't just inefficiency.

21
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:21,680
It's a body count. A J.A.M.A. study broke down two hundred and forty-seven point three billion dollars in N.I.H.

22
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:27,420
spending (twenty ten to twenty nineteen). Each of these categories funds valuable work.

23
00:02:27,940 --> 00:02:33,740
The question is whether the ratio makes sense when thousands of safe compounds sit untested.

24
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:40,560
eighty-four point nine percent goes to basic research (two hundred and nine point nine billion dollars), which involves

25
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:44,180
understanding disease mechanisms, molecular biology, and genomics.

26
00:02:44,860 --> 00:02:51,440
Essential for identifying new targets, but eighty-five cents of every dollar is a lot of understanding and not much testing.

27
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:58,440
eleven point eight percent goes to applied research that is not clinical trials (twenty-nine point three billion dollars),

28
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:04,360
translating discoveries toward practical use, but without the final step of testing them in patients.

29
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:12,160
The Clinical and Translational Science Awards, or C.T.S.A. infrastructure program, provides about one billion dollars a

30
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:19,400
year in trial infrastructure, coordination, and training. Observational studies involve population health data collection

31
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:26,880
(All of Us: two point one six billion dollars), which are valuable for identifying patterns, but observation without intervention

32
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:34,600
doesn't cure anyone. Other applied research includes drug characterization, health services research, and epidemiology.

33
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:42,240
three point three percent goes to phased clinical trials (eight point one billion dollars), which is the only category that

34
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:46,340
directly tests whether treatments work in humans (Phases one through three).

35
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:52,860
Phase one accounts for one point five billion dollars (eighteen point five percent of clinical trial budget).

36
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:57,820
Phase two accounts for three point five billion dollars (forty-three point two percent).

37
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:02,500
Phase three accounts for two point six billion dollars (thirty-two point one percent).

38
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:10,380
The N.I.H. covers only three point seven to four point three percent of Phase three costs, leaving completion to industry,

39
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:13,780
which only funds trials with patent-protected profit potential.

40
00:04:14,860 --> 00:04:22,120
Other or unclassified trials account for zero point five billion dollars (six point two percent) for cross-phase overhead,

41
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:25,740
pilot studies, and trials not classified to a single phase.

42
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:33,260
Pragmatic trials (eighty-two times cheaper per patient) are severely underfunded despite proven efficiency.

43
00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:39,180
Why This Allocation Exists. This isn't bureaucratic accident.

44
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:47,420
It's lobbied outcome. Drug companies love the N.I.H. Taxpayers absorb the risk of basic research; industry patents whatever

45
00:04:47,420 --> 00:04:53,440
works. It's like if your neighbor paid for your kitchen renovation, and then you charged them rent to eat there.

46
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:58,060
But independent trials comparing drugs head-to-head? Those are terrifying.

47
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:03,820
They might reveal that the eighty thousand dollar drug works about as well as the four dollar generic.

48
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:09,320
PhRMA, the pharmaceutical lobby, has consistently opposed comparative effectiveness research.

49
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:17,920
When Congress created the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute, or P.C.O.R.I., in twenty ten, industry successfully

50
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:22,580
lobbied to prohibit it from making coverage recommendations based on cost-effectiveness.

51
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:28,760
Congress literally passed a law saying "don't fund research that might lower drug prices."

52
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:33,920
They wrote it down. In legislation. On purpose. With their names on it.

53
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:39,880
And then they went home and told their constituents they were fighting for affordable healthcare, because your species'

54
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,480
capacity for doublethink is genuinely Olympian. The revolving door helps.

55
00:05:45,540 --> 00:05:48,780
N.I.H. advisory committees include industry representatives.

56
00:05:49,020 --> 00:05:54,580
Former N.I.H. officials join pharmaceutical boards. Academic researchers depend on industry grants.

57
00:05:55,100 --> 00:06:02,040
Everyone's incentives align against cheap, fast trials testing whether expensive drugs outperform generics.

58
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:07,150
It's not a conspiracy. Conspiracies require secrecy. This is all public.

59
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:14,980
It's just that everyone involved happens to benefit from the same terrible outcome, purely by coincidence, repeatedly, for

60
00:06:14,980 --> 00:06:20,620
decades. On Wishonia, we have a word for this. The word is "conspiracy."

61
00:06:20,960 --> 00:06:26,410
The difference between a conspiracy and a system is whether the participants need to meet in a dark room.

62
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:31,140
These people meet in conference rooms with catering. The outcome is identical.

63
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:36,160
Basic research produces publications. Publications produce tenure.

64
00:06:36,580 --> 00:06:41,540
Tenure produces more grants. Nobody in this cycle needs anyone to get better.

65
00:06:41,980 --> 00:06:45,500
They need papers. Papers are the currency of academic science.

66
00:06:46,180 --> 00:06:53,960
Cures are a byproduct that occasionally happens when someone forgets to optimize for papers and accidentally optimizes for

67
00:06:53,960 --> 00:07:01,460
results instead. This has happened approximately fifty times per year out of two point five million papers.

68
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:08,440
The rest of the papers cite each other. The three point three percent allocation isn't a mystery.

69
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:14,160
It's a business model. The Efficiency Gap: A Tale of Two Trials.

70
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:20,340
The difference between efficient and inefficient trial design isn't theoretical.

71
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:25,040
Humanity accidentally ran a controlled experiment on itself during COVID.

72
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:29,440
Two countries. Same disease. One spent like it was trying to cure death.

73
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:35,220
The other actually did. RECOVER Initiative (N.I.H. Approach).

74
00:07:35,380 --> 00:07:43,240
The N.I.H. spent one point six six five billion dollars (one point one five billion dollars plus five hundred and fifteen

75
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:47,840
million dollars in twenty twenty-four) (source) over four years and counting.

76
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:52,940
Enrolled about thirty thousand patients. Completed zero trials.

77
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:56,720
Cost per patient: fifty-five thousand five hundred dollars.

78
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:04,610
That's the price of a luxury SUV per person, and at the end of it, nobody got a luxury SUV and nobody got cured.

79
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:10,920
They got observed. Expensively. RECOVERY Trial (U.K. Approach).

80
00:08:11,420 --> 00:08:16,740
The British spent twenty million dollars over six months. Enrolled forty-eight thousand patients.

81
00:08:16,980 --> 00:08:23,480
Found multiple effective treatments, including dexamethasone, which saved over one million lives.

82
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:27,260
Cost per patient: five hundred dollars. That's the price of a nice dinner.

83
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:37,179
The N.I.H. spent one hundred and eleven times more per patient and achieved infinitely less (dividing by zero trials completed

84
00:08:37,179 --> 00:08:41,679
produces infinity, which is fitting because that's how long patients waited).

85
00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:51,060
This efficiency gap is not unique to RECOVERY. A systematic review of sixty-four pragmatic trials found a median cost of

86
00:08:51,060 --> 00:08:57,700
ninety-seven dollars per patient. The British cured disease for the price of a nice dinner per patient.

87
00:08:58,420 --> 00:09:03,300
America spent the price of a luxury SUV per patient and cured nothing.

88
00:09:04,120 --> 00:09:09,220
The problem isn't that curing disease is hard. The N.I.H. doesn't have a capability problem.

89
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:16,820
It has a shopping addiction. Specifically, it's addicted to buying infrastructure, coordination, training, observation,

90
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:23,560
and overhead, which are all things you buy before curing disease, the same way you buy pots and pans before cooking dinner,

91
00:09:24,160 --> 00:09:30,200
except the N.I.H. has been buying pots and pans for fifty years and has not yet started cooking.

92
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:39,320
The Death Toll: Opportunity Cost of Misallocation. When you misallocate the vast majority of a forty-seven billion dollar

93
00:09:39,320 --> 00:09:46,280
annual budget into low-efficiency research instead of high-efficiency clinical trials, you aren't just wasting money.

94
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:53,060
You are composting people. Cost Per Q.A.L.Y. (Quality-Adjusted Life Year).

95
00:09:53,940 --> 00:10:01,800
The standard N.I.H. portfolio produces one Q.A.L.Y. for fifty thousand dollars at standard medical research return on investment,

96
00:10:02,340 --> 00:10:08,540
or R.O.I. (and that's being generous). Pragmatic platform trials produce one Q.A.L.Y.

97
00:10:08,580 --> 00:10:16,860
for four dollars. Every one dollar spent on efficient pragmatic trials buys twelve thousand five hundred times more health

98
00:10:17,220 --> 00:10:25,100
than a dollar spent on the current N.I.H. portfolio. The ADAPTABLE trial proved pragmatic trials cost forty-four point one

99
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:32,660
times less per patient than traditional randomized controlled trials, or R.C.T.s (nine hundred and twenty-nine dollars versus

100
00:10:32,660 --> 00:10:37,460
forty-one thousand dollars). Same science. Fewer catered meetings.

101
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:41,220
Same number of patients helped. Fewer conference rooms rented.

102
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:50,160
The "Death Equivalent" of Budget Misallocation. Annual opportunity cost: approximately one hundred million Q.A.L.Y.s and

103
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:59,060
approximately seven million death-equivalents per year. Even the most conservative estimate: approximately ten million Q.A.L.Y.s

104
00:10:59,380 --> 00:11:04,060
and approximately seven hundred thousand preventable deaths annually.

105
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:11,630
The efficiency gap is twelve thousand five hundred times. For every dollar spent on pragmatic trials producing Q.A.L.Y.s,

106
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:16,140
the N.I.H. spends that many dollars to achieve the same result.

107
00:11:16,780 --> 00:11:24,240
If your car ran at zero point zero zero eight percent efficiency, it would move one inch per gallon.

108
00:11:24,700 --> 00:11:30,780
If your heart pumped at zero point zero zero eight percent efficiency, you'd be dead before finishing this sentence.

109
00:11:31,560 --> 00:11:37,520
Your medical research system runs at zero point zero zero eight percent efficiency, and you gave it a raise.

110
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:41,440
You gave it a raise and a new building. The building has a cafeteria.

111
00:11:42,920 --> 00:11:49,260
The Translation Crisis: All Theory, No Medicine. The problem is not a shortage of ideas.

112
00:11:49,640 --> 00:11:56,720
It is a shortage of anyone bothering to test them. There are over twenty thousand F.D.A.-approved drugs that are known to

113
00:11:56,720 --> 00:12:02,880
be safe in humans. There are roughly four thousand seven hundred to six thousand eight hundred clinically-tested candidates

114
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:09,160
gathering dust, including three thousand four hundred and twenty-two marketed drugs that could be repurposed for diseases

115
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:14,240
nobody's tried them on. There are hundreds of plausible dementia interventions.

116
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:17,840
And the number of clinical trials available for your grandma in St.

117
00:12:17,940 --> 00:12:25,120
Louis? Zero. You have a warehouse of safe molecules that have never been systematically tested for new uses.

118
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:30,440
The warehouse is full. The testing facility is closed for a team-building retreat.

119
00:12:31,080 --> 00:12:36,720
The N.I.H. is, by its own metrics, a publishing house that occasionally dabbles in medicine.

120
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:41,240
It publishes two point five million funded papers annually.

121
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:45,280
It produces approximately fifty new drug approvals annually.

122
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:51,520
That's a conversion rate from paper to patient of zero point zero zero two percent.

123
00:12:52,540 --> 00:13:00,940
For every fifty thousand papers, one drug reaches a human. The rest become citations in other papers, which become citations

124
00:13:00,940 --> 00:13:05,640
in other papers, in an infinite loop of academics reading each other's homework.

125
00:13:06,240 --> 00:13:12,820
On Wishonia, we call this a "knowledge ouroboros." It looks productive from the inside.

126
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:18,260
From the outside, it's a snake eating its own tail and calling it lunch.

127
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:25,940
Plenty of Knowledge. No Translation. About five percent of patients have participated in clinical trials.

128
00:13:26,780 --> 00:13:30,240
About forty-four point eight percent would participate if invited.

129
00:13:30,780 --> 00:13:38,240
About nine to eleven percent have ever been invited. Nearly half of all patients would join a trial if asked.

130
00:13:38,780 --> 00:13:46,880
Almost nobody asks. Your species has a room full of volunteers holding their hands up, and a system that looked at the room

131
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:52,540
and decided to study the clipboard instead. The clipboard is very well-studied.

132
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:57,380
It has been studied for decades. Multiple papers have been written about the clipboard.

133
00:13:57,900 --> 00:14:06,840
The volunteers are still holding their hands up. Understanding disease and curing disease are completely different activities.

134
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:12,640
One requires microscopes. The other requires giving people medicine and seeing if they stop dying.

135
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:20,920
The N.I.H. chose microscopes. It chose them fifty years ago and has not reconsidered since, because reconsidering would

136
00:14:20,920 --> 00:14:27,840
require a committee, and the committee would need funding, and the funding would come from the same budget that's already

137
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:35,900
ninety-six point seven percent allocated to things that aren't curing disease, and so the committee would conclude that

138
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:41,900
committees are important, and the microscopes would stay. The Public Goods vs.

139
00:14:42,160 --> 00:14:48,320
Club Goods Scam. The N.I.H. claims to produce "public goods" like open science and training.

140
00:14:48,700 --> 00:14:55,560
What it actually produces is a free R. and D. department for pharmaceutical companies, funded by the same people who then

141
00:14:55,560 --> 00:15:04,020
pay full price for the drugs. Taxpayers funded research behind ninety-nine percent of new drugs (three hundred and fifty-six

142
00:15:04,020 --> 00:15:08,460
drugs, twenty ten to twenty nineteen). Industry patents the winners.

143
00:15:08,840 --> 00:15:15,340
The public pays again through high drug prices. When research leads to a dead end, the public eats the loss.

144
00:15:16,120 --> 00:15:22,640
You pay for the lottery ticket. They cash the winnings. You'd think someone would have mentioned this arrangement before

145
00:15:22,640 --> 00:15:30,400
now, and someone has, many times, but the people who cash the winnings also fund the campaigns of the people who could change

146
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:36,020
the arrangement, so the arrangement continues. The only things the N.I.H.

147
00:15:36,020 --> 00:15:44,140
*should* fund are things private companies *cannot* profitably fund: open pragmatic trial platforms that are eighty-two

148
00:15:44,140 --> 00:15:49,680
times cheaper per patient than traditional trials yet receive minimal N.I.H.

149
00:15:49,820 --> 00:15:57,460
funding, repurposing of generic drugs (more than four thousand seven hundred clinically-tested compounds where no patent

150
00:15:57,500 --> 00:16:06,640
means no profit means no interest), and comparative effectiveness data from head-to-head trials revealing which drugs actually

151
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:14,040
work best (pharma's nightmare, your potential salvation). These are the only things the N.I.H.

152
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:20,400
should be funding. Instead, the N.I.H. explicitly avoids them to "not crowd out industry."

153
00:16:21,190 --> 00:16:28,080
Translation: "We won't run the trials that would lower drug prices, because the people who set drug prices asked us nicely."

154
00:16:29,080 --> 00:16:35,600
They asked nicely and also spent one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars on lobbying, but the niceness was noted.

155
00:16:36,680 --> 00:16:40,820
The Patient Disconnect: Zero Correlation with Health Outcomes.

156
00:16:41,520 --> 00:16:47,320
Correlation between N.I.H. funding priorities and actual disease burden: zero point zero seven.

157
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:54,720
That is not a typo. On a scale of zero (random) to one (perfect), the N.I.H.

158
00:16:54,740 --> 00:17:00,920
scores "basically a coin flip." A dartboard would allocate research funding more rationally.

159
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:01,280
A drunk person throwing darts at a list of diseases would produce a funding allocation that correlates more closely with

160
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:07,020
actual disease burden than the National Institutes of Health, which employs twenty thousand people and has been doing this

161
00:17:07,020 --> 00:17:11,680
for fifty years. The dartboard doesn't even need to be in the building.

162
00:17:12,839 --> 00:17:20,119
Patients want medicine that works, access to experimental therapies, trials they can join, and a cure before they die.

163
00:17:20,680 --> 00:17:28,400
The N.I.H. funds molecular mechanisms, papers in prestigious journals, research empires, and institutional overhead.

164
00:17:28,540 --> 00:17:32,570
These two lists do not overlap. They don't even live in the same neighborhood.

165
00:17:33,180 --> 00:17:38,220
The patients are over here, dying. The N.I.H. is over there, publishing.

166
00:17:38,680 --> 00:17:43,940
The distance between them is measured in papers. Patients don't control the money.

167
00:17:44,440 --> 00:17:49,140
Committees do. And committees optimize for the continued existence of committees.

168
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:53,580
This is true of all committees, everywhere, since the invention of committees.

169
00:17:54,260 --> 00:17:58,060
Nobody has ever convened a committee that recommended fewer committees.

170
00:17:58,460 --> 00:18:06,420
The Track Record. Since nineteen seventy, the N.I.H. has spent over one point one trillion dollars (inflation-adjusted).

171
00:18:07,140 --> 00:18:12,920
Diseases eradicated: Zero. This isn't because eradicating disease is impossible.

172
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:16,900
The W.H.O. eradicated smallpox for three hundred million dollars.

173
00:18:17,720 --> 00:18:25,180
Jonas Salk developed the polio vaccine in a university lab and gave it away for free, which is why nobody at the N.I.H.

174
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:32,160
talks about him at parties because he makes everyone look bad by comparison and also he's dead, which is the thing they

175
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:38,560
were supposed to be fixing. Veterinarians have eradicated multiple animal diseases while the N.I.H.

176
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:44,680
was forming subcommittees about forming subcommittees. Cows have better healthcare outcomes than you do.

177
00:18:45,540 --> 00:18:53,060
Cows. The animals you eat for dinner have a more effective disease eradication program than the species that eats them.

178
00:18:53,960 --> 00:19:01,300
This is the single most embarrassing sentence in this manual, and this manual contains many embarrassing sentences, most

179
00:19:01,300 --> 00:19:06,880
of them about your species. What Would Actually Work. The U.K.

180
00:19:06,880 --> 00:19:13,100
RECOVERY trial already proved it. Five hundred dollars per patient instead of forty-one thousand dollars.

181
00:19:13,720 --> 00:19:18,840
First life-saving result in under one hundred days. Over one million lives saved.

182
00:19:19,540 --> 00:19:24,560
The blueprint exists. It is not theoretical. It happened. In England.

183
00:19:24,900 --> 00:19:29,240
During a pandemic. While Americans were arguing about horse dewormer.

184
00:19:30,380 --> 00:19:34,720
The evidence is right there. It's not hiding. It's waving at you.

185
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:41,370
It has been waving at you for years. The recipe is simple enough that I can state it in four sentences.

186
00:19:42,380 --> 00:19:47,260
Pay for results: treatment equals payment, no treatment equals no payment.

187
00:19:48,220 --> 00:19:56,320
Publish everything, including failures, especially failures (because failures are the only data your species systematically

188
00:19:56,320 --> 00:20:05,660
destroys, which means they're the data you need most). Eliminate gatekeepers and let patients vote with participation, because

189
00:20:05,660 --> 00:20:12,460
the people who will die if the system fails are more motivated than the people who get tenure if the system continues.

190
00:20:13,780 --> 00:20:21,580
Automate administration with transparent systems instead of committees, because committees cannot be trusted (see above,

191
00:20:21,880 --> 00:20:28,480
and also see the entire history of committees, and also see the committee that was formed to investigate whether committees

192
00:20:28,480 --> 00:20:32,420
can be trusted, which concluded that committees are essential).
